  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on self-reported survey data from a single European capital, which may limit generalizability to cities with different socio-political contexts or green space planning histories.",
      "what": "This study analyzed changes in how urban green space users interact with and perceive biodiversity by comparing survey data from 1,217 visitors across five socially diverse parks in Paris between 2010 and 2023. The analysis reveals that park users increasingly link their personal well-being to the perceived richness of local biodiversity, show a higher demand for ecologically oriented green spaces, and express a growing preference for community-led conservation over public policy initiatives.",
      "why": "It documents a decade-long shift in public expectations, demonstrating that urban residents' definition of green space quality has evolved from simple recreational access to active ecological richness, while their trust in municipal environmental management has simultaneously eroded."
    }
  },
